natambio (1.1.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * NAE: new <pan_scale>, a width control on the engine's input pair -- a
    power-complementary mid/side matrix applied before anything looks at the
    signal, ahead of the decomposition and of the correlation the beta mode
    weighting rides on. +1 collapses the pair to mono, 0 leaves it untouched,
    -1 puts the two channels in opposite polarity. Read in both modes. An
    exploratory control: it promises no improvement in quality, and leaving it
    at 0 is a perfectly good answer.
  * NAE: internal naming reworked around the two components (c1, c2), and the
    beta mode output called ambient rather than surround.
  * XTC: new <frac_delay> in <xtc> and <xtc_asym>, which runs the crosstalk
    recursion at the exact ITD in the frequency domain instead of rounding it
    to whole samples. The rounding capped the design's own suppression in the
    top octaves -- at 48 kHz an itd_us of 180 is 8.64 samples and became 9,
    around -27 dB above 8 kHz, where the exact ITD reaches the ladder's floor
    some 55 dB lower. It matters more in <xtc_asym>, which rounds each side
    independently, so the round-trip period inherits both errors. Optional
    and default false, so existing configurations keep producing the same
    coefficients.
  * XTC: new <model_delay> (default 64 samples), the bulk delay the fractional
    path adds to all its filters so that a two-sided fractional shift is not
    clipped at n = 0. Ignored unless <frac_delay>; given without it, it now
    warns instead of being read and silently dropped.
  * XTC: the <xtc>/<xtc_asym> configuration dump reports what each ITD becomes
    in samples and which of the two paths is in use, and <xtc_asym> also
    reports the round-trip period, where the two per-side roundings compound.
  * XTC: an unknown tag inside an <xtc_asym> <left>/<right> side block is now
    a parse error. <frac_delay> and <model_delay> describe the whole design
    and belong to the <xtc_asym> block itself; tried inside a side block they
    previously did nothing at all, and said nothing about it.
  * New <remote> block: a TCP command socket, one command per line, that moves
    gains and NAE parameters while natambio runs. The tag's content is the
    port; there is no default, so a configuration that says nothing about
    remote control gets no socket. natambio refuses to start if the port
    cannot be bound. Bound to 127.0.0.1, and the commands carry no credential
    of any kind, so driving it from another machine means an SSH forward.
  * Remote: "up" and "down" move named ports' gains relative to what they are
    now, "upin"/"downin"/"upout"/"downout" move every input or every output
    port bodily, and "get" reports the gains as they stand. Results are
    clamped to [-120, +20] dB and slewed at the start/stop fade rate rather
    than applied as a step. A command that fails changes and reports nothing.
  * Remote: "mute" takes every output to -120 dB and leaves the ports' own
    gains alone, so up/down go on working through it; "unmute" brings them
    back and "toggle" is the two of them for a caller with a single button.
    The input ports are deliberately left running, so the convolution tails
    and the NAE window are not drained into silence.
  * Remote: "naegain" sets an NAE engine's <front_gain>, <ambience_gain> and
    <rear_gain> by the engine's <name>, and "naepan" its <pan_scale>. Both
    are absolute rather than relative, being a balance and a width rather
    than a volume; naepan refuses a value outside [-1, +1] instead of
    quietly clamping it.
  * Remote: "naeget" reports one engine's configuration as its XML block, and
    "getxmlconfig" the whole live configuration, valid to start another
    natambio with. Every command that changes something writes the new value
    back into the configuration as well as into the running audio, so there
    is one place holding what the system is set to.
  * The <port>s of <jack_input> and <jack_output> take a <gain> in dB
    (default 0 dB, following the common gain convention), which is what the
    remote's gain commands move.

 -- Raul Fernandez Ortega <natambio.audio@gmail.com>  Wed, 02 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0200

natambio (1.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Initial Debian packaging.
  * Split into natambio (real-time JACK client), natambio-tools (offline C
    FIR/DRC generators), natambio-ladspa (LADSPA plugin) and natambio-drc
    (offline Python toolkit).

